Interview Question:
Difference between NFS and SMB?
NFS is common in Linux environments.
SMB is common in Windows environments.

NetApp Angle: NFS storage is one of NetApp's core offerings.

SMB (Server Message Block)
SMB is basically NFS for Windows. Windows file sharing protocol.
Example:
You open: \\fileserver\HR
That's SMB.

Why Used in Corporate Offices?
Supports:
Windows login
Active Directory
Permissions

Features:
File sharing
Printer sharing
Authentication
Access control
Common Usage

Example:
HR Folder
HR team -> Full access
Developers -> No access

Windows handles this easily via SMB.

NFS vs SMB:
Imagine two countries.
NFS: Speaks Linux language.
SMB: Speaks Windows language.
Both do file sharing.
Different ecosystems.

